hi there this is steve kaufman again uh  i'm going to do a little uh video here  on memory  this is a request from one of my viewers  wakako  um what do i consider to be the role of  memory in language learning  i'd like to start with an example that i  got from  oscar one of our link members in spain  we just had a conversation in spanish  and we talked about memory and he said  you know it's a bit like driving a car  when we drive a car  we do everything automatically because  we have  basically ingrained the the  the habits the patterns all the things  we need to do in order to drive a car we  do them automatically and we can't  remember  the last 10 minutes of our ride what we  did how we  you know signaled or turned or breaked  or changed gears or whatever it might be  and i think language learning is a bit  like that let me think of some other  things that relate to memory um i read  somewhere that they did a test with a  group of two groups of 20 language  learners  and the first  group was given a list of  whatever 20 30 words to learn  to study and the second group was not  given that list then they were asked to  read a text  and there was no difference in the  ability of these two groups to read  this text this was in the language that  they were learning and and so i mean  what was being pointed out in that study  was that being confronted with a lot of  things to remember  we don't necessarily remember them very  well  um  you know i have the experience when i  read sometimes for my russian study i'll  read a play by czechov  and at the beginning of the play they  introduce all of the  people who are in the play the general  and his daughter and so and so and his  wife and and of course as soon as i  start reading the play i can't remember  who's who  i know that when i look up words in a  dictionary uh no sooner do i look up the  words then i've forgotten what the  meaning was  so i think memory is not a very good  thing to rely on when speaking  sort of to review because it's a bit  like driving the car you have to be able  to produce these words and phrases  automatically on the fly  when we're driving i don't think we rely  on memory i don't think we try to  remember something we read in a manual  um  you know when i'm reading the play it's  only after i've read the play  quite a way through the play and i start  to become comfortable with the  characters in the play  occasionally referring back to the list  of characters that that i become  comfortable with who who is who in the  play  similarly with  with  words you know it's only when i've seen  the words in different contexts that  they start to stick  so  i think what we're dealing with in  language learning is more a matter of  establishing certain patterns in the  brain  so that initially things are new  um  primarily words and phrases and  gradually these become  patterns uh and the way the patterns are  formed is either  it's i think it's a combination of  of uh you know the brain  kind of uh  absorbing a lot of input and starting to  form its own patterns based on what it  hears  but i think the brain also  tries to rely on some logic that the  brain has  and one good example is  my favorite example of the third person  singular in the present tense of english  it's the only one that changes i go you  go he goes  uh he went they went uh you know but  he talks she talks so that always  changes  and yet i would think that amongst  even some very proficient speakers of  english even some native speakers of  english  he don't go  um  you know  people continue to make that mistake  because the the brain is saying  the pattern is that this should be the  same as all the others  and it's very difficult to remember by a  memory  that the third person singular is the  only one that's different  so i see language learning more as a  matter of  of gradually  getting used to something and allowing  the brain to form the necessary patterns  and this requires a lot of exposure
 

